@title CI Provider Comparison
@subtitle Replacing Jenkins for the firmware and cloud repos
@author Developer Experience, Ridgeback Robotics

We run about 38000 build minutes per month for 120 engineers. Some jobs need ARM runners, and some need the hardware-in-the-loop lab. We compared GitHub Actions, CircleCI and Buildkite.

# Comparison

table = Provider,Median build time (min),ARM runners,Self-hosted agents,Monthly cost; GitHub Actions,14.2,yes,yes,"$2,860"; CircleCI,11.8,yes,yes,"$3,420"; Buildkite,9.6,via our own agents,yes (required),"$2,150 plus our compute"

# Benchmarks

data bench = pipeline,GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite; firmware-build,21.4,17.9,13.2; cloud-api-tests,9.8,8.1,7.4; docs-site,3.1,2.7,2.9
chart bar bench x=pipeline y=GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite unit=min labels title="Median build time by reference pipeline"

# Pros and cons

### GitHub Actions

- Pro: ARM runners and self-hosted agents are both available.
- Pro: sits next to our code in GitHub.
- Con: the slowest of the three on every reference pipeline.

### CircleCI

- Pro: faster than GitHub Actions on all three reference pipelines.
- Con: the highest monthly cost.

### Buildkite

- Pro: the fastest median build time, at 9.6 minutes.
- Pro: the lowest monthly cost before our compute.
- Con: ARM and hardware-in-the-loop work runs on our own agents.

# Recommendation

! Buildkite, because the hardware-in-the-loop lab needs self-hosted agents anyway.

!! Buildkite moves runner patching onto the DevEx on-call, estimated at 6 hours a month.
